Let an event handler run a nested event loop on X11 (SQ8L modal menus and dialogs).

puglUpdate() refuses to run while events are being dispatched, so a modal loop started
from a mouse handler (the editor's popup menus and dialogs, drawn in the plugin window on
Linux) could not process the window's events. Allow it, as the Windows backend does in
its recursive loops: the nested call dispatches and exposes, then restores the state.

diff --git a/src/x11.c b/src/x11.c
index 84a6fa3..4e03228 100644
--- a/src/x11.c
+++ b/src/x11.c
@@ -1848,7 +1848,10 @@ puglUpdate(PuglWorld* const world, const double timeout)
 
   if (startState == PUGL_WORLD_IDLE) {
     world->state = PUGL_WORLD_UPDATING;
-  } else if (startState != PUGL_WORLD_RECURSING) {
+  } else if (startState != PUGL_WORLD_RECURSING &&
+             startState != PUGL_WORLD_UPDATING) {
+    // (SQ8L) an event handler may run a nested loop (modal menus and dialogs drawn in
+    // the window): process events recursively, like the Windows and macOS modal loops.
     return PUGL_BAD_CALL;
   }
 
